<body><main>
<header id="top"><h1>Njord: data, model and benchmark analysis</h1><p>A practical explanation of the current implementation and what the experiments establish.</p><p class="meta">English report · Updated 25 November 2026 · Our archived runs, Nils’s supplied results and inspected code</p></header>
<nav aria-label="Chapters"><a href="#data">1. Data versions</a><a href="#model">2. Model and message passing</a><a href="#results">3. Results</a><a href="#next">4. Next steps</a></nav>
<p class="caption" id="table-emphasis-key"><strong>Table highlights — bold:</strong> key facts or references. <strong class="value-best">Green</strong> / <strong class="value-worst">red:</strong> more / less favorable observed values within the stated comparison group. Colors do not indicate correctness or statistical significance.</p>

<section class="chapter" id="data"><h2>1. Data versions</h2>
<p>We use the <strong>same scientific ocean data</strong> in two storage representations. The difference is when we select ocean points and assign them to ranks. A rank is a process controlling one GPU in these runs.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table id="data-versions-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Version A: original full store</th><th>Version B: prepared ocean shards</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Stored spatial points</td><td><strong>61,200</strong> rectangular grid cells, including land.</td><td><strong>42,348 valid ocean points</strong> in total; <strong>42,348 / 21,174 / 10,587 per rank</strong> with 1 / 2 / 4 GPUs.</td></tr>
<tr><td>When selection happens</td><td>The original sample-building path materializes <strong>full-grid data, then selects ocean points</strong>.</td><td>An offline tool selects ocean points and writes each rank’s store <strong>before the run</strong>. Sample building still happens during training.</td></tr>
<tr><td>What is preserved</td><td colspan="2"><strong>Ocean values, global node IDs, normalization statistics, masks and target definitions.</strong> We do not compute separate normalization statistics for each GPU.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Relevant runs</td><td><strong>4981:</strong> original model, full store.</td><td><strong>5031:</strong> original model, one ocean shard. <strong>4944 / 4949 / 4952:</strong> distributed implementation, 1 / 2 / 4 ranks.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>1.1 How we built the benchmark dataset</h3>
<p>We started with <strong>31 daily snapshots from 1–31 January 2023</strong>, for both ocean and atmosphere. The preparation script writes that <strong>complete 31-step sequence 16 times</strong>—the original sequence plus 15 copies—into <code>physical_repeat_16</code>. This gives <strong>31 × 16 = 496 stored time positions</strong>. It repeats the sequence, rather than repeating each individual day 16 times.</p>
<figure><div class="figure-scroll">@@REPEAT_DATASET_GRAPH@@</div><figcaption>Each block contains the same source days 1–31. Numbers under the block labels are positions in the expanded store: positions 32–62 reuse source days 1–31, and so on. This is dataset construction, not 16 training epochs.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The script shifts timestamps forward continuously, from <strong>1 January 2023 to 10 May 2024</strong>, and writes independent Zarr time chunks. The physical values and stored forcing values—including day-of-year features—repeat; static fields, masks and normalization statistics are retained. These are <strong>16 copies of a 31-day block, not 16 independent observed months</strong>. The purpose is a larger physical-data I/O benchmark, not additional scientific training data.</p>
<h3>1.2 How we built Version B</h3>
<p>Starting from this full repeated store, we apply the existing surface-ocean mask: <strong>61,200 cells → 42,348 ocean points</strong>. Geographic RCB assigns each ocean point to one rank. We then write a paired <code>ocean.zarr</code> and <code>atmosphere.zarr</code> for every rank, with the source values, masks and statistics for its points.</p>
<figure><div class="figure-scroll">@@VERSION_B_GRAPH@@</div><figcaption>Only the spatial layout changes. Each alternative retains all 496 time positions. The one-, two- and four-GPU layouts are separate stored alternatives; a run selects its matching layout.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Within one layout, each ocean point has <strong>exactly one owner</strong>. We preserve its original full-grid ID and ocean-node ID alongside a local contiguous index. The build validates source-value equality, coordinate alignment and complete, disjoint ownership. Halo feature copies are exchanged during model execution; they are not extra ocean points permanently duplicated in these shards.</p>
<details><summary>What is saved, and how the time split is handled</summary>
<p>Each layout contains <code>manifest.json</code>, <code>surface_owners.npy</code> and <code>rank_0/</code>, <code>rank_1/</code>, etc. Each rank directory contains its two Zarr stores. Coordinates <code>global_grid_index</code> and <code>global_surface_index</code> map local positions back to the source and graph. Time chunks contain one timestamp and all locally owned points.</p>
<p>The repeat builder keeps the original validation/test lengths (8 and 9 time positions), puts them at the end of the new timeline and uses the remaining <strong>479 positions for training</strong>. The configured AR4 dataset yields <strong>473 usable training windows</strong>. Original normalization statistics are copied rather than recomputed over the expanded timeline. Repeated patterns and artificial block boundaries mean these splits are suitable for this performance proxy, not independent forecast-skill evidence.</p>
</details>
<h4>Where work happens when a run loads a batch</h4>
<p><strong>Original path:</strong></p><div class="flow" aria-label="Original data path"><span class="box">Full Zarr chunks<small>61,200 cells / time</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Decode, normalize,<br>construct sample</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Ocean selection,<br>masks + ice channel</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Ocean batch → GPU</span></div>
<p><strong>Prepared-shard path:</strong></p><div class="flow" aria-label="Prepared shard data path"><span class="box">Offline: select ocean<br>and owner IDs once</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Write rank stores</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">During run: decode,<br>normalize, build sample</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Owned batch → GPU</span></div>
<p>The original source is already training-ready Zarr, rather than raw observations. Preparing shards changes the storage layout; it does <strong>not</strong> replace the remaining loader transformations or preload the whole dataset into memory.</p>
<details><summary>Data contents, sample sizes and Zarr chunks</summary>
<p>There are 27 physical state variables: sea-surface height; ice concentration and thickness; temperature, salinity and two current components at six depths. A derived binary ice-presence channel makes 28 model state channels.</p>
<p>Seven static features describe bathymetry, mean dynamic topography, coast distance and sine/cosine encodings of longitude and latitude. Two cyclic time features and eight atmospheric variables are windowed over three times, yielding six and 24 channels per forecast step.</p>
<p>For global batch size two, histories have shape <code>[2, 2, N, 28]</code> and targets <code>[2, 4, N, 28]</code>. Here N is the local number of ocean points. The condition has <code>2×28 + 6 + 7 + 24 = 93</code> channels per point. All effective model inputs are float32.</p>
<p>The original state chunks are <code>[27 features, 1 time, 61200 cells]</code> in float64; atmospheric chunks are <code>[8, 1, 61200]</code> in float32. Indexing a few cells may still decode the whole spatial chunk. Shards store the complete local spatial extent per time.</p>
</details>

<h3>1.3 Ways to load these representations</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table id="loading-options-table"><thead><tr><th>Loading method</th><th>How it works</th><th>Status / trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>One GPU, original loader</td><td>CPU workers construct a full ocean batch, then copy it to one GPU. It can use either the full store or the one-rank ocean store.</td><td>Measured in <strong>4981 and 5031</strong>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Each rank loads its shard</td><td>Ranks use the same time-sample sequence and load different spatial subsets. Each GPU receives only its own batch.</td><td><strong>Current MP runs. No full-batch broadcast.</strong> Stores must match ownership.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rank 0 loads and broadcasts</td><td>Rank 0 constructs the batch and broadcasts the full GPU batch; each rank selects its nodes in forward.</td><td><strong>Implemented fallback / earlier prototype.</strong> Central input path and replicated batches; separate from graph halos.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shared CPU reader, targeted distribution</td><td>Decode source chunks once, use the ownership plan to send only the required slices to each rank.</td><td><strong>Proposed.</strong> Useful for today’s all-grid chunks; producer throughput and extra CPU copies need measurement.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ranks index the original source</td><td>Use each rank’s node IDs before materialization, without writing GPU-count-specific stores.</td><td><strong>Proposed adapter.</strong> Flexible, but several ranks may decode the same large chunk.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Spatial chunks / bounded decoded cache</td><td>Reuse smaller tiles or a size-limited cache across different ownership plans.</td><td><strong>Proposed.</strong> Better suited to large datasets than whole-dataset RAM preload; requires identity and capacity checks.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>1.4 Workers, prefetch and temporary storage</h3>
<p>Current AR4 runs use <strong>8 workers per rank and prefetch 2</strong>: 8 / 16 / 32 workers in total on 1 / 2 / 4 GPUs. Prefetch 2 permits up to two pending batches per worker. More queue slots do not by themselves make decoding faster.</p>
<div class="flow"><span class="box">CPU workers prepare batch n+1<small>while GPU computes batch n</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Trainer receives CPU batch</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Copy to GPU and wait</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Wait for other ranks;<br>start next graph step</span></div>
<p>Workers use <code>spawn</code> and persist between batches. In the measured path, <code>pin_memory=False</code>; copies use <code>non_blocking=True</code> followed by a CUDA wait. CPU preparation can overlap compute, but GPU copies are not a double-buffered pipeline here.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Four different operations:</strong> staging copies compressed files to job-local <code>$TMPDIR</code>; spatial preprocessing writes a new layout; worker prefetch prepares future batches; RAM preload keeps decoded values resident. In these single-node jobs, ranks share a job staging root—GPUs do not each have their own temporary disk.</div>
<p>All ranks process the <strong>same dates and global batch of two</strong>. They own different places, not different samples. The loader and graph must use the same global node IDs and ownership plan.</p>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="model"><h2>2. Model: where message passing happens, before and now</h2>
<p><strong>The original model already performs graph message passing.</strong> Our implementation distributes those same graph operations across spatial partitions and adds communication when a receiver needs a sender owned by another rank.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table id="model-comparison-table"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Before: original GraphEFM</th><th>Now: distributed execution</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Graph and learned transformations</td><td>Grid, two meshes, edge MLPs and node MLPs execute on one GPU.</td><td><strong>Same topology, formulas and model complexity.</strong> Nodes and receiver-owned edges are partitioned.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sender features</td><td>Every sender is already on the device.</td><td>Local senders are available; <strong>remote sender features arrive as temporary halo copies</strong>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Aggregation</td><td>Combine all incoming edge messages for each receiver.</td><td>The receiver owner still holds all incoming edges and performs the <strong>same complete mean or sum</strong>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Autoregressive feedback</td><td>Keep the complete predicted ocean state on one GPU.</td><td>Each rank keeps its own predicted state for the next step. <strong>No full-grid gather</strong> between forecasts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backward</td><td>Ordinary autograd; checkpointing recomputes activations.</td><td>Also <strong>return halo gradients</strong> to sender owners and <strong>sum gradients for replicated model weights</strong>. Replay must use a consistent communication schedule.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>2.1 One training forecast, in execution order</h3>
<p>The two grid MLPs assemble representations <strong>before</strong> graph processing. History-only inputs have 93 channels; adding the target’s 28 channels creates a separate 121-channel posterior input. Both are embedded to width 64. Coarser meshes have fewer nodes, but still 64 features per node.</p>
<div class="route"><strong>A. Posterior: target-aware path · 4 graph applications</strong><div class="flow"><span class="box">121 → 64 MLP</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Grid → mesh 0<small>mean</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Mesh 0 ×1<small>sum</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Up to mesh 1<small>mean</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Mesh 1 ×1<small>sum</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">q(μ, σ) → sample z</span></div></div>
<div class="route"><strong>B. Decoder: its own traversal using history and z · 10 graph applications</strong><div class="flow"><span class="box">History embedding</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Grid → mesh 0<small>mean</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Mesh 0 ×2<small>sum</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Up to mesh 1<small>sum; receiver starts from MLP(z)</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Mesh 1 ×2<small>sum</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Down to mesh 0<small>mean; saved fine states</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Mesh 0 ×2<small>sum</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Mesh → grid<small>sum; grid skip path</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">64 → 28 MLP<small>state increment</small></span></div></div>
<div class="route"><strong>C. Prior: history-only path for the KL term · 4 graph applications</strong><div class="flow"><span class="box">93 → 64 MLP<small>embedding shared with decoder</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Grid → mesh 0<small>mean</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Mesh 0 ×1<small>sum</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Up to mesh 1<small>mean</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">Mesh 1 ×1<small>sum</small></span><span class="arrow">→</span><span class="box">p(μ, σ = 1)</span></div></div>
<p>Training uses <strong>18 graph applications per forecast and 72 across AR4</strong>. Each branch sends fine information upward once, then performs its coarse layers; it does not resend mesh 0 before every mesh-1 layer. The decoder receives the sampled latent z rather than continuing the posterior’s hidden mesh states.</p>
<p><strong>Inference:</strong> history → prior → sample z → decoder → prediction. There is no target or posterior: 14 graph applications per forecast, 56 across four steps per ensemble member. Different latent draws produce different scenarios.</p>
<h3>2.2 Which nodes exchange information?</h3>
<p>The saved graph contains <strong>42,348 grid nodes, 8,456 mesh-0 nodes and 2,097 mesh-1 nodes</strong>. Select a relation below to inspect a small schematic. It illustrates ownership and edge directions; it is not the actual saved graph.</p>
<div class="controls"><label for="graph-relation">Relation:</label><select id="graph-relation"><option value="g2m">Grid → mesh 0</option><option value="m0">Within mesh 0</option><option value="up">Mesh 0 → mesh 1</option><option value="m1">Within mesh 1</option><option value="down">Mesh 1 → mesh 0</option><option value="m2g">Mesh 0 → grid</option></select></div>
<figure><div class="figure-scroll">@@MODEL_GRAPH@@</div><figcaption id="graph-caption">Grid → mesh 0: sender grid embeddings are aggregated at fine-mesh receivers. Blue/green nodes have different owners; orange edges need halos.</figcaption></figure>
<p class="scroll-note">Wide diagrams and tables can be scrolled sideways.</p>
@@RELATION_TABLE@@
<div class="note"><strong>A graph message is not the same as a GPU transfer.</strong> An edge MLP computes a message from sender, receiver and edge features. A halo exchange first makes remote sender features available. The receiver owner then computes the same edge MLP as before.</div>
<details><summary>Every graph call, in exact training order</summary>@@TRACE_TABLE@@<p class="caption">Order, relation counts and aggregation were traced with hooks on the actual H64 model using one synthetic CPU forecast. This checks structure, not performance or forecast skill.</p></details>
<details><summary>MLPs, aggregation formulas and residual connections</summary>
<p>Grid condition MLP: <code>93 → 64 → 64</code>; posterior grid MLP: <code>121 → 64 → 64</code>. Separate mesh and edge geometry MLPs use <code>4 → 64 → 64</code>. After G2M, each branch applies a pointwise SiLU + Linear 64 → 64 projection.</p>
<p>For every edge, concatenate <code>[edge(64), sender(64), receiver(64)]</code>. The edge MLP is <code>192 → 64 → 64</code>; after aggregation, the receiver-update MLP is <code>128 → 64 → 64</code>.</p>
<div class="formula">Propagation: G2M, encoder/prior up, decoder down<br>m = h_sender + EdgeMLP([e, h_sender, h_receiver])<br>a = mean(all incoming m)<br>h_receiver_new = a + NodeMLP([h_receiver, a])</div>
<div class="formula">Interaction: within meshes and decoder up<br>m = EdgeMLP([e, h_sender, h_receiver])<br>a = sum(all incoming m)<br>h_receiver_new = h_receiver + NodeMLP([h_receiver, a])</div>
<p>Within-mesh layers also return <code>e_new = e + m</code>. These updated edges feed later layers; saved fine states and edges are reused on descent. Cross-level edge embeddings are not updated. M2G uses the interaction-style sum and receiver residual without updating edges.</p>
<p>The decoder embeds z with <code>64 → 64 → 64</code>, builds a grid skip <code>h + MLP(h)</code>, applies a 64 → 64 projection before M2G and a final <code>64 → 64 → 28</code> increment map. Standard hidden MLPs use Linear → SiLU → Linear → LayerNorm; distribution and final-output maps omit the last LayerNorm.</p>
</details>
<h3>2.3 How a message crosses the GPU boundary</h3>
<figure><div class="figure-scroll">@@HALO_GRAPH@@</div><figcaption>Example: one sender owned by rank 0 supplies two receiver edges on rank 1. Its feature vector is transferred once per peer and relation, then reused.</figcaption></figure>
<ol><li><strong>Gather:</strong> select unique remote sender IDs with <code>index_select</code> and pack their current features.</li><li><strong>Send / receive:</strong> post grouped <code>isend</code> / <code>irecv</code> operations with <code>batch_isend_irecv</code>, then wait for completion. Peer transfers can run concurrently.</li><li><strong>Compute locally:</strong> concatenate owned and halo sender states, run the edge MLP for each local receiver edge, aggregate all messages, then update receivers.</li><li><strong>Return gradients:</strong> autograd sums uses of a halo copy, sends its gradient to the owner, and the owner adds it with <code>index_add</code>.</li><li><strong>Synchronize weights:</strong> sum replicated parameter gradients with <code>all_reduce(SUM)</code> before AdamW. Spatial contributions are summed, not averaged over ranks.</li></ol>
<p>For batch size 2, width 64 and float32, one distinct remote sender costs 512 feature bytes per peer per exchange, before protocol overhead. Current code waits for halos before the graph operator; useful local edge computation is <strong>not yet deliberately overlapped</strong> with those transfers.</p>
<details><summary>Topology, real partitions, latent distributions and training loss</summary>
<p>Graph creation uses coordinates rather than the 93 physical-input channels. The cluster builder forms mesh 0 with spherical K-means and forms the next level from preceding mesh positions. Spherical Delaunay gives candidate same-level edges, followed by configured filters. G2M uses a radius neighbourhood; the builder default is 0.67 × mean fine-mesh chord-edge length, a chosen hyperparameter. M2G selects nearest mesh nodes (requested degree three); fine-to-coarse links choose a nearest coarse node and reverse for descent. The benchmark loads saved graph files; their exact generation command is not recorded.</p>
<p>The measured geographic RCB baseline balances each layer independently: south/north for two ranks, then an unwrapped-longitude split at four. It is not the older fixed −5° cut. Equal grid counts do not imply equal mesh/edge/halo cost.</p>
<div class="map-grid"><figure>@@MAP_TWO@@<figcaption>Actual grid coordinates, sampled; two owners.</figcaption></figure><figure>@@MAP_FOUR@@<figcaption>Same sampled coordinates; four owners.</figcaption></figure></div>
@@OWNER_TABLE@@
<p>The posterior predicts a diagonal Gaussian on 2,097 coarse nodes, with 64 means and 64 scales per node; scales are <code>softplus(raw) + 10⁻⁴</code>. The prior learns means and fixes standard deviation to one in this configuration. Sampling occurs after the encoder’s messages; it does not replace aggregation.</p>
<p>The objective is weighted reconstruction error + β·KL(q||p), with β = 1 and CRPS weight zero. It sums time, spatial and feature/latent contributions and averages the batch. Output constraints and ice-density feedback are applied before using predictions as later history. The four forecasts are connected without detaching.</p>
<p>MP checkpoint replay executes another 72 forward graph applications in backward, plus reverse halo gradients. Early stopping is disabled in MP so ranks replay a consistent communication schedule; the original reference enables it. Latent noise is drawn outside replay and selected by global coarse-node IDs. Full scenario inference throughput is not measured here.</p>
</details>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="results"><h2>3. Results</h2>
<p>The current series keeps H64, decoder L2, encoder/prior L1, global batch 2, AR4, FP32, AdamW and checkpointing. Each case has 10 warmup and 50 measured optimizer steps: 100 measured samples. Workers are 8 per rank, with prefetch 2. These are performance windows, not convergence experiments.</p>
@@CURRENT_TABLE@@
<p class="caption">Rate, batch-time and memory colors mark extrema across these five observed cases. In the speedup column, MP1 is the neutral reference; green/red compare MP2 with MP4.</p>
<h3>3.1 What did the data control change?</h3>
<p><strong>4981 → 5031:</strong> the original model improves from 1.062 to 1.197 samples/s when using the prepared ocean store: +12.8% observed throughput. <strong>5031 → MP1:</strong> the remaining difference is +4.1%, rather than the earlier +17.5% comparison against the full-store reference.</p>
<figure><div class="figure-scroll">@@THROUGHPUT_PLOT@@</div><figcaption>Observed samples divided by the complete measured wall window. Higher is faster. All five current cases are shown.</figcaption></figure>
<p>A data-layout change can also affect forward/backward intervals: background CPU preparation competes with the training process, and event intervals can include launch gaps. The remaining one-GPU difference is not an isolated implementation gain: checkpoint replay and preflight-batch lifetime still differ, and there is only one independent job per case.</p>
<h3>3.2 Where is the time spent now?</h3>
<figure><div class="figure-scroll">@@PHASE_PLOT@@</div><figcaption>Additive breakdown: select one critical rank per step. Forward includes forward halos; backward includes replay and reverse halos. Parameter-gradient synchronization is separate.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Visible input accounts for about 6.3% of original/full, 5.5% of original/ocean and 2–4% of MP time.</p>
<div class="note"><code>data_read_ms</code> measures the trainer’s wait for the CPU batch, including preparation when it is not already prefetched. <code>host_to_device_ms</code> covers copying and waiting. <code>data_rank_wait_ms</code> covers rank readiness. Graph halo traffic is inside forward/backward, rather than these loading timers. A small visible wait does not mean CPU decoding was free.</div>
<p><strong>MP2 → MP4:</strong> mean batch time increases by about 78 ms. On the additive critical-rank timeline this is mainly +63 ms backward, +12 ms forward and +7 ms weight synchronization, partly offset by 8 ms less input time. This identifies where to investigate; it does not prove whether packing, CPU scheduling, small kernels, replay or imbalance dominates.</p>
<h3>3.3 Throughput, memory and correctness</h3>
<figure><div class="figure-scroll">@@MEMORY_PLOT@@</div><figcaption>Peak allocated GPU memory per device. This includes live input and activation buffers; reserved allocator memory is a different metric.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Relative to MP1, two GPUs achieve 1.353× speedup and 67.6% efficiency; four achieve 1.269× and 31.7%. MP2 is fastest at this model size. MP4 reduces memory per GPU to 0.965 GiB, despite taking longer per batch.</p>
<p>Allocated GPU-seconds/sample are 0.802 / 1.186 / 2.529 for MP1 / MP2 / MP4, excluding startup. Thus MP2 favors latency, while MP1 favors GPU-time efficiency at H64. This is not a billing formula.</p>
<p>The 50 measured scalar losses are exactly equal for original/full, original/ocean and MP1. Maximum relative deviations are 2.18×10⁻⁷ for MP2 and 1.82×10⁻⁷ for MP4. Small-graph CUDA/NCCL tests additionally compare predictions, gradients and optimizer updates. This is strong implementation evidence, rather than a long-horizon scientific skill assessment.</p>
<details><summary>Exact phase means, startup and comparison limitations</summary>@@PHASE_TABLE@@<p class="caption">Timing colors compare the five current cases; rank-readiness wait, weight synchronization and metric-reduction colors compare MP2 with MP4 only. Equal zero broadcast times are emphasized neutrally.</p>@@STARTUP_TABLE@@
<p>Published multi-rank phase means maximize each phase independently across ranks. Do not sum them: different maxima can come from different ranks, and compute/step/end-to-end are nested intervals. The stacked plot instead uses one critical-rank record per step.</p>
<p>All five current runs used the same node and GPU model, but separate allocations and physical GPU UUIDs. There are no independent repetitions yet. Staging, initialization and cache construction are excluded from steady-state throughput; 5031 stages both original metadata/source and its shard.</p>
<p>The MP runner keeps the preflight batch alive during measurement, while the original runner replaces it. One global batch is about 93 MiB; MP1’s peak is about 89 MiB higher than the original’s. This is a relevant buffer-lifetime difference, not an exact allocation attribution.</p>
</details>
@@PNEMO_RESULTS@@
@@HALO_CPU_RESULTS@@
<h3 id="ar1-history">3.6 Exploratory AR1 tests — historical context only</h3>
<p>The earlier one-step runs were useful for checking communication and exploring loaders, workers and prefetching. <strong>AR1 is too light to represent our target AR4 training workload:</strong> it unrolls one forecast instead of four, reducing autoregressive compute, activation and replay work. This does not necessarily mean a different spatial architecture.</p>
<p>In that lighter setup, visible input could take roughly 90% of a step; more workers helped some configurations and hurt others. These are interesting engineering observations, but <strong>they are not used to judge the full model’s scaling or select its final loader settings</strong>. The performance conclusions and next steps above are based on the current AR4 series.</p>
<div class="two-col compact-plots">
<figure><h4>Visible data time versus model time</h4><div class="figure-scroll">@@AR1_DATA_PLOT@@</div><figcaption>AR1 only. Percentages beside job IDs show visible input’s share of total batch time. “Model step” includes forward, backward/replay, weight synchronization, optimizer and metrics.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><h4>Inside the model step</h4><div class="figure-scroll">@@AR1_MODEL_PLOT@@</div><figcaption>Same six runs, with input excluded. The separate time scale makes forward, backward/replay and the remaining step overhead visible.</figcaption></figure>
</div>
<p>On one GPU, moving from zero to two workers reduced visible input from <strong>1,411 to 486 ms</strong>, while the model step changed only slightly: <strong>224 → 221 ms</strong>. On four GPUs, the two-worker run was slower in both input (<strong>1,085 → 2,174 ms</strong>) and model-step time (<strong>117 → 831 ms</strong>). This is consistent with host-side contention or scheduling effects, but these timers alone do not establish the cause.</p>
<p class="caption">All six jobs used the same node and 238 measured AR1 steps. Workers are per rank; the two-worker runs use prefetch 2. These are separate historical jobs, not independent repetitions of a controlled worker experiment. The additive plots use one critical-rank record per step, so components can differ from the archive’s independently maximized phase means. “Data time” is visible input wait/copy/distribution, not all background CPU work; “model time” is elapsed step time, not pure GPU arithmetic.</p>
<details><summary>Exact AR1 timing values behind these two plots</summary>@@AR1_TIMING_TABLE@@<p class="caption">Green/red compare zero versus two workers at the same GPU count. Input percentages are bold for context and are not ranked as an optimization objective.</p></details>
<details><summary>Archive: exploratory AR1 measurements and separate smoke checks</summary>
<p>The archive preserves the earlier results for traceability. AR length and test purpose are listed explicitly; AR4 single-step smoke measurements are functional checks, separate from the AR1 experiments.</p>
<ul><li><strong>4177 / 4178:</strong> first AR1 prototype with full-batch distribution and a replicated front end.</li><li><strong>4310 / 4311 / 4312:</strong> 238 AR1 steps, zero workers: 1.221 / 1.474 / 1.660 samples/s.</li><li><strong>4347 / 4344 / 4348:</strong> 238 AR1 steps, two workers/rank, prefetch 2: 2.814 / 2.960 / 0.667 samples/s.</li><li><strong>4341 → 4342:</strong> two GPUs, one worker, prefetch 2 → 4: 1.861 → 1.953 samples/s. Two workers/P2 reached 2.954 in 4343 and 2.960 in the longer 4344 run.</li><li><strong>4352 / 4354:</strong> four GPUs with one worker/P4 stayed slow; zero-worker 4354 reached 1.610 on a different node. That cross-node comparison does not isolate workers.</li></ul>
@@HISTORY_TABLE@@
<p class="caption">All 23 earlier real-data MP measurement/smoke JSONs available in this archive. Throughput colors apply only to the six 238-step AR1 cases and compare worker policies at the same GPU count; other workloads and smoke rates are not ranked. Different workloads are not pooled with current AR4. Unrecorded prefetch is not evidence of disabled prefetch. Single-step smoke runs are functional checks rather than steady-state benchmarks.</p>
</details>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="next"><h2>4. Next steps and relation to the team’s work</h2>
<p><strong>Latest priority after job 6131:</strong> stabilize and simplify the runtime/reference experiment before changing model width, checkpointing or storage. Section 3.5 separates the large setup-level improvement from the smaller paired transport effect. The original live-input cases and exploratory AR1 archive remain historical context.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table id="next-steps-table"><thead><tr><th>Order</th><th>Work and reason</th><th>Team connection</th><th>Completion criterion</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Explain the startup restriction. Record masks around imports, CUDA, eager/lazy NCCL and the first collective, with the actually loaded NCCL version.</td><td>Request Nils’s saved initial/post-init affinity and runtime metadata.</td><td>Locate narrowing in a trace; confirm or reject the version-specific NCCL lead.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Choose the minimal CPU policy. Within one four-GPU allocation, compare the shared full CPU pool with disjoint pairs; keep model and transport fixed.</td><td>The launcher owns placement; Slurm owns allocated resources.</td><td>Determine whether fixed pairs add value or verified full-pool access is sufficient.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Freeze the reference. Match graph, owners, batches, weights, H64/AR4/B2, numerical policy and timer; complete matching one-/two-GPU cases.</td><td>Original/global-model and max remain the anchor; distinguish original-versus-MP validation from P2P-versus-PN validation.</td><td>Forecast/loss/gradient agreement and a controlled 1/2/4-GPU comparison.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Resolve the small transport difference. Job 6131 reports 3.34% PN gain; individual pairs give 1.51%, 10.11%, 1.89%. Use at least four counterbalanced pairs if it drives a design decision.</td><td>Both transports now share our model, graph, partition and input.</td><td>Repeatability and candidate-order sensitivity assessed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Clean up around the frozen experiment. One configuration, launcher-owned placement, selectable transport and compact canonical reporting. Audit duplicated training logic before replacing it.</td><td>Review Nils’s original-layer adapter and Lassi’s ownership interface.</td><td>Correctness tests pass; refactoring preserves the scientific workload and input.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Then investigate additional performance choices: numerical checks, owner maps, overlap, width/checkpoint capacity and live I/O, each separately.</td><td>Nils’s capacity results, Lassi’s partition/profiling work and Alex’s replay-safe overlap remain independent directions.</td><td>One changed factor per comparison; timing scope and preparation cost stated.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>4.1 What the other branches contribute</h3>
@@TEAM_TABLE@@
@@TEAM_UPDATE@@
<p>Alex partitions mesh processing while keeping grid/G2M/M2G work replicated; our current path partitions these as well. These are alternative runtime boundaries, so integration needs an explicit choice rather than simply combining both implementations.</p>
<p>The earlier Lassi/Nils rows describe the pinned branch review through 24 November; they are not a claim about every newer branch. The new <code>nils-pnemo</code> row reflects the 25 November inspection and supplied slides. Nils’s October profiling used another GPU; his new PhysicsNeMo results use the same GTX 1080 Ti GPU model as ours, but a different mesh and benchmark policy.</p>
<h3>4.2 When to revisit loading and model size</h3>
<p>After one ownership interface exists, compare shared CPU reading, direct indexed reading and validated derived caches at a fixed CPU budget. Every backend must return identical local tensors. Record cache construction and staging separately as well as steady-state throughput. Current all-grid chunks make repeated independent decoding a real risk.</p>
<p>Run separately labelled width and checkpoint sweeps to find the scaling and capacity crossover. Nils’s wider cases now provide a reason to test this, with independent repetitions still needed. A larger model changes compute-to-communication balance and may hide input preparation; it does not by itself repair an inefficient pipeline or establish better forecast accuracy.</p>
